\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Maximum Empirical Likelihood Estimation (MELE)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Abstract

\series bold
\size normal
Estimation of Standard Linear Model - Maximum Empirical Likelihood Estimator:
 
\series default
Combination of the idea of maximum likelihood method of moments, Maximum
 Entropy Empirical Likelihood Estimator, Univariate Informational Measure,
 Relative Entropy, Kullback Leibler Distance (Discrepancy), Kullback-Leibler
 Information Cirterion, Cressie-Read Distance (Discrepancy), 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha$
\end_inset

-Entropy, Generalized Empirical Likelihood (GEL)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Maximum Empirical Likelihood Estimation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{t},\ t=1,2,\ldots,n,$
\end_inset

 be a random sample from the population distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 The parametric maximum likelihood estimation requires the knowledge of
 the density function, and maximizes the log likelihood function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\ln\ L={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln\ f(y_{t};\theta})
\]

\end_inset

The knowledge of the density function implies the knowledge about the relationsh
ip between the parameters and the density function.
 Consider a discrete Bernoulli distirbution:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{t};\theta)=\theta^{y_{t}}(1-\theta)^{1-y_{t}},\ y_{t}=0\ \mbox{or}\ 1
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}\equiv f(y_{t};\theta)=P(y_{t}=1)\ \mbox{or}\ P(y_{t}=0)$
\end_inset

, we may consider the ML estimation as a constrained maximization
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{p_{t}}\ \sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln(p_{t})\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ p_{t}=\theta^{y_{t}}(1-\theta)^{1-y_{t}}}
\]

\end_inset

The distribtuion function cotnains all statistical properties of a random
 variable, including moments, shape, etc.
 Suppose that we do not have complete informaiton contained in the distribution
 function, but have only information about the moments.
 Can we still apply the same optimization procedure, replacing the density
 function with moment conditions as the constraints, i.e., 
\series bold
unbiased estimating function
\series default
? For example,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{p_{t}}}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln\ (p_{t})\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ p_{t}>0,\ E(y_{t}-\theta)=0}
\]

\end_inset

Note that we require a strict positivity for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 Otherwise, the joint density will be zero.
 In fact, this condition is already embedded in the object funciton because
 the object funciton will be negative infinity if one of the 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 is zero.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once problem with this formulation is that the objective function is not
 bounded, while the density function is bounded.
 We need a normatilization constraint on 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

.
 We will use 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}=1$
\end_inset

 as the normalization.
 Another problem is how to compute the moment condition without knowing
 the distribution funciton.
 The MME computes it by the sample mean:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{t}-\theta)=0\implies{\displaystyle \frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}(y_{t}-\theta)=0}
\]

\end_inset

This is equivalent to using 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{1}{n})$
\end_inset

 as the probability for each observation.
 The empirical likelihood estimator uses the empirical probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 in the place of 
\begin_inset Formula $(\frac{1}{n})$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(y_{t}-\theta)=0\implies{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta)=0}
\]

\end_inset

Under these two modifications we can pose the problem as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{p_{t}}}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ln(p_{t})\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ p_{t}>0,{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}=1,\ \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta)=0\label{eq:1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

When we introduce the moment information we need to consider the following
 Lagrange equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L=\sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln(p_{t})-\kappa\left({\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}-1\right)+\lambda\left(\sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta)\right)\label{eq:2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

First order conditions are nonlinear in the parameters and Lagrange multiplies,
 and we cannot find the closed form solutions.
 However, from the first order conditions, we can still derive important
 results
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial p_{t}}=0 & \iff & \frac{1}{p_{t}}-\kappa+\lambda(y_{t}-\theta)=0\label{eq:3}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\kappa}=0 & \iff & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}=1\label{eq:4}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\lambda}=0 & \iff & \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta)=0\label{eq:5}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Consider equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:3"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{p_{t}}-\kappa+\lambda(y_{t}-\theta) & = & 0\nonumber \\
1-\kappa p_{t}+\lambda p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta) & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(multiplied both sides by \ensuremath{p_{t}})}\nonumber \\
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}1-\kappa{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}+\lambda{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}(y_{t}-\theta)} & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(summing both sides over \ensuremath{t})}\nonumber \\
n-\kappa & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(by equations (\ref{eq:4}) and (\ref{eq:5}))}\nonumber \\
 & \implies & \hat{\kappa}=n\label{eq:6}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Using the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:6"

\end_inset

), in equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:3"

\end_inset

), we obtain an estimate for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{p_{t}}-n+\lambda(y_{t}-\theta) & = & 0\nonumber \\
 & \implies & \hat{p_{t}}=\frac{1}{n+\hat{\lambda}(y_{t}-\theta)}\label{eq:7}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

When we plug this estimate for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

,(
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:7"

\end_inset

), into equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:5"

\end_inset

), we obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\lambda}$
\end_inset

 as the solution too:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\lambda}\equiv{\displaystyle \arg_{\lambda}}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ \frac{(y_{t}-\theta)}{n+\hat{\lambda}(y_{t}-\theta)}=0\label{eq:8}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This can be written as a (
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

-1)th order polynomial equation in 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

.
 The estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 can then be found from the maximization of the concentrated objective function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \max_{\theta}\ }{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ln(\hat{p_{t}})\label{eq:9}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The example above is the case of only one empirical moment.
 The MELE of distribution parameter 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 in a general form can be summarized as follows.
 We wish to maximize the empirical likelihood function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \max_{p_{t}}\ \ln(L)={\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln(p_{t})}}
\]

\end_inset

subject to the strict positivity, normalization, and unbaised estimating
 function
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{t}>0,\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}=1,\ E(h_{t}(\theta))\equiv E[h(y_{t};\theta)]=0}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{t}(\theta)\equiv h(y_{t};\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times1$
\end_inset

 vector for each 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 Since the true distirbution function is unknown, this is computed by empirical
 probability
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E(h_{t}(\theta))=0\implies\sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}h_{t}(\theta)=0
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the empirical Lagrange equaiton can be written as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L=\frac{1}{n}\sum_{t=1}^{n}\ln(p_{t})-\kappa\left(\sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}-1\right)+\lambda^{\prime}\sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}h_{t}(\theta)\label{eq:10}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

-dimensional vector of Lagrange multipliers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Once again, from the first order conditions, we can derive important results:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial p_{t}}=0 & \iff & \frac{1}{np_{t}}-\kappa+\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)=0\label{eq:11}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\kappa}=0 & \iff & {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}=1\label{eq:12}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\lambda^{\prime}}=0 & \iff & \sum_{t=1}^{n}p_{t}h_{t}(\theta)=0\label{eq:13}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Consider equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:11"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{np_{t}}-\kappa+\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta) & = & 0\nonumber \\
\frac{1}{n}-\kappa p_{t}+\lambda^{\prime}p_{t}h_{t}(\theta) & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(multiplied both sides by \ensuremath{p_{t}})}\nonumber \\
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\frac{1}{n}-\kappa{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}p_{t}+{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}\lambda^{\prime}p_{t}h_{t}(\theta)} & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(summing both sides over \ensuremath{t})}\nonumber \\
n-\kappa & = & 0\ \ \mbox{(by equations (\ref{eq:12}) and (\ref{eq:13}))}\nonumber \\
 & \implies & \hat{\kappa}=1\label{eq:14}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Using the estimate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\kappa$
\end_inset

, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:14"

\end_inset

), in equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:11"

\end_inset

), we can derive the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{1}{np_{t}}-1+\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta) & = & 0\nonumber \\
 & \implies & \hat{p_{t}}=\frac{1}{n\left[1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right]}\label{eq:15}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

By plugging in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:15"

\end_inset

) into equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:13"

\end_inset

), the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 for a given 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is given implicity by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ \frac{h_{t}(\theta)}{1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)}=0\label{eq:16}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituing the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{t}$
\end_inset

 into the empirical log-likelihood function, we can write a concentrated
 likelihood function as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\mbox{ln}(L)=\sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}(p_{t})=-\sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}\left(n\left[1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right]\right)=-\sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}(n)-\sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}\left(1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right)\label{eq:17}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Therefore, the original maximization problem is equivalent to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \min_{\theta}\ }\sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}\left(1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right)\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ {\displaystyle \sum_{t=1}^{n}}\ \frac{h_{t}(\theta)}{1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)}=0\label{eq:18}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the constraint equation that defines the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 implicitly is the same as the first order condition of the maximization
 of the following equation with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{\lambda}\ \sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}\left(1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right)\label{eq:19}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Hence, we can write the problem in compact form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\theta}_{MEL}\equiv\arg\min_{\theta}\ \max_{\lambda}\ \sum_{t=1}^{n}\mbox{ln}\left(1-\lambda^{\prime}h_{t}(\theta)\right)\label{eq:20}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Maximum Entropy Empirical Likelihood Estimator
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Univariate Informational Measure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Suppose we have a set of events 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

with a probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 If we receive a message that a certain event has occured, what will be
 a reasonable measure of the informational content of such a message? Shannon
 measured it by 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln(\frac{1}{p_{i}})=-\ln(p_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

 is low (e.g., 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}=0.01$
\end_inset

), 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not expected to occur often.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

 did occure, the news of such an occurence is a surprise and contains a
 great deal of information.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This measure is not symmetric in the snese that the message of occurence
 and the message of non-occurence give different values 
\begin_inset Formula $\ln(\frac{1}{p_{i}})\ \mbox{and}\ \ln(\frac{1}{1-p_{i}})$
\end_inset

.
 When 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

 is low, the message of the occurence of 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a big surprise, but the message of non-occurence is not much of a surprise.
 Before we receive a message, the expected informational content of the
 message is called 
\series bold
Shannon's entropy
\series default
.
 When we have 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 events with probabilities 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

, Shannon's entropy is expressed as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
SE(p)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}\ln(\frac{1}{p_{i}})=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}\ln(p_{i})\label{eq:21}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

The 
\series bold
maximum entropy empirical likelihood (MEEL)
\series default
 estimator maximizes this objective function subject to the normalization
 and empirical moments constraints:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{p_{i}}\ \left(-\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}\ln(p_{i})\right)\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ \sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}=1,\ \sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}h_{i}(\theta)=0\label{eq:22}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 is a 
\begin_inset Formula $m\times1$
\end_inset

 vector.
 Setting up the Lagrange equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L=-\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}\ln(p_{i})-\kappa\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}-1\right)+\lambda^{\prime}\sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}h_{i}(\theta)\label{eq:23}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

From the first order conditions, we can derive important results:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial p_{t}}=0 & \iff & -(\ln(p_{i})+1)-\kappa+\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)=0\label{eq:24}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\kappa}=0 & \iff & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p_{i}=1\label{eq:25}\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial\lambda^{\prime}}=0 & \iff & \sum_{i=1}^{n}p_{i}h_{i}(\theta)=0\label{eq:26}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

From equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:24"

\end_inset

), we can obtain an expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
-(\ln(p_{i})+1)-\kappa+\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta) & = & 0\\
\ln(p_{i}) & = & \lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)-\kappa+1\\
 & \implies & p_{i}=\exp\left[\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)-\left(\kappa+1\right)\right]
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking this expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

, we now substitute it in (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:25"

\end_inset

):
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}}{e^{\kappa+1}}=1\implies e^{\kappa+1}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the explic solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

 is given by:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{p}_{i}(\theta,\lambda)=\frac{e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}}{{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}}\label{eq:27}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Now, substituting the expression for 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{i}$
\end_inset

, (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:27"

\end_inset

), into equation (
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand ref
reference "eq:26"

\end_inset

), we derive a solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\frac{{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}h_{i}(\theta)}{{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}} & = & 0\nonumber \\
 & \implies & \hat{\lambda}\equiv\lambda(\theta)=\sum_{i=1}^{n}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}h_{i}(\theta)=0\label{eq:28}
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Therefore, the original maximization problem is equivalent to
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\max_{p_{i}}\ \left(-\sum_{i=1}^{n}\hat{p}_{i}\ln(\hat{p}_{i})\right)\ \ \mbox{s.t.}\ \frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}h_{i}(\theta)}{{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}}=0\label{eq:29}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the constraint equation that defines the solution for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 implicitly is the same as the first order condition of the minimization
 of the following equation with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\min_{\lambda}\ \ln\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}\right)\label{eq:30}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Hence, we can write the problem in compact form as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\hat{\theta}_{MEEL}\equiv\arg\max_{\theta}\ \min_{\lambda}\ \ln\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n}e^{\lambda^{\prime}h_{i}(\theta)}\right)\label{eq:31}
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

